# Agent discipline (applies to every session)

You are executing ONE lane file from `plans/`. The executor may be Cursor
Composer or Claude Code — these rules apply to either. Rules:

1. **Read your assigned `plans/W*.md` file first, fully.** It is the contract.
   Also skim `plans/PLAN.md` and the sibling lanes named in your "Depends on"
   so you build against the right contracts.
2. **Work on a branch named `agents/<lane-id>`** (e.g. `agents/w11-territory`).
   Create it as your first action: `git checkout -b agents/<lane-id>`. If the
   worktree gave you an auto-generated branch name, rename it:
   `git branch -m agents/<lane-id>`. Never work directly on `main`.
3. **Touch only files in your lane's allowlist.** If a fix seems to require
   editing a file outside it, STOP and report — do not improvise.
4. **One commit per checklist item.** The message uses a **conventional-commit
   prefix** with the lane/item marker at the END in parentheses:
   `<type>(<scope>): <summary> (W<lane>.T<item>)`
   e.g. `feat(map): static neon border effect (W11.T1)`.
   Valid `<type>`: feat, fix, docs, refactor, test, chore. `<scope>` is the
   layer (map, manim, broll, infra, compose, assets, orchestration…).
   **NEVER use `W<lane>.T<item>` as the prefix** — the prefix is always the
   conventional type. Plain `git commit -m` only. NEVER add `Co-authored-by:`
   or any attribution trailer, never modify git user.name/user.email.
5. **You never touch GitHub (executor role).** No `git push`, no PRs, no
   remotes. Commit locally; the operator reviews, merges, and pushes. Ending
   your session with unpushed local commits on your branch is the CORRECT final
   state. (Exception: if you ARE the lead — the user explicitly directs you to
   merge/push — that is a lead action, not an executor one.)
6. **Run the acceptance command(s) before declaring an item done.** Paste the
   output (or describe the rendered artifact) in your report. If a render or
   test needs secrets and `.env` is missing here, link it:
   `ln -sf /home/rawline/GeoPoAI/.env ./.env`.
7. **Never refactor opportunistically.** Out-of-scope cleanups are forbidden
   even if they look easy.
8. **Schemas in `docs/contracts/` and `config/design_tokens.json` are frozen.**
   If your task seems to need a schema change, STOP and report.
9. All visual constants (colors, fonts, glow, timing, safe areas) come from
   `config/design_tokens.json` — never hardcode hex values or font names.
   Provisioned asset dirs are `assets/{icons,font,sfx,model}/` (the `kind`
   names from the manifest schema — note `font`/`model` are singular).
10. New assets (icons, fonts, SFX, models) enter only via
    `tools/prepare_assets.py` and `assets/manifest.json` — never download ad
    hoc. Every asset entry needs a license field.
11. No placeholders, no half-finished stubs, no shortcuts: whatever the
    acceptance step says to download, render, or verify must actually be
    downloaded, rendered, and verified — for real.
12. If a checklist item is ambiguous, implement the narrowest reading and flag
    the ambiguity in your report rather than expanding scope.
13. New user-facing surface (actions, effects, params, CLI flags) gets
    documented in your layer's `docs/fragments/<lane>.md` — never edit
    SKILL.md directly; the lead merges fragments at integration.
14. **Effects animate in AND out.** Every visual effect gets a deliberate,
    token-timed entrance and exit (use `timing` tokens incl. `exit_ratio`) —
    never an instant pop unless the scene explicitly asks for a cut. Any effect
    that can persist on screen ships a `remove*`/`hide*` action so it can leave
    cleanly. "Static/instant" is not a substitute for a designed entrance; if a
    lane file omits the in/out behavior, implement a deliberate one and flag it.
15. Final report format: per item — what changed, how verified, open concerns.
